A||||9||85ZainoGT||whats the difference???||||4||
Z||000000||85ZainoGT||10-18-2001||10:24 PM||||86 vs 86.5 gt?  what is the diff? please tell tell tell!!!<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-18-2001||10:37 PM||||There is no difference.  An '86 GT is an '86.5 GT and vice versa.  It just depends on how you like to name it.......sort of like the '64 1/2 Mustang......it came out late in '64 and people just started calling it '64 1/2 and the name stuck.<P>It is a ".5" because it wasn't relased until June of 1986.....most cars are first introduced in Sept. of the previous year, so it was in fact, a mid-year car.  For example......my '88 GT was built in Oct. 1987 (it just turned 14!)......if no '88 Gts were built until June of 1988, then we could call it an '88.5.<P>I hate the ".5" moniker......it just confuses people.  All GT's in 1986 were '86 models.....period (forget about the .5).     <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||85ZainoGT||10-18-2001||10:40 PM||||ok thanks.. lol... I kinda figured that was what it was, just makin sure.. was buggin me!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tonker||10-19-2001||12:05 AM||||Didn't they start producing GT's with the 5 spd halfway through the production year for the 86's?||||reg||5
Z||000004||Mach10||10-19-2001||12:22 AM||||Ya. the 86.5s had the Getrag 5spd...||||reg||1
Z||000005||SLYGUY||10-19-2001||12:39 AM||||From what I've been reading the main distinction between 86 and 86.5 is in fact the 5 speed Getrag. <BR>Glad to hear it confirmed.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||10-19-2001||02:07 AM||||Out of 17,660 1986 GT's made, Only 435 had the 5-spd. All other manual transmissions had the 4-spd. The 86 GT was called the 86 1/2 GT because it came out in mid-year. (May was the first delivery month) Hence the 1/2 (or.5) designation. If the 1/2 was to distinguish the 5-spd, how come there is no such thing as an 86 1/2 SE?||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||10-19-2001||12:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Out of 17,660 1986 GT's made, Only 435 had the 5-spd. All other manual transmissions had the 4-spd. The 86 GT was called the 86 1/2 GT because it came out in mid-year. (May was the first delivery month) Hence the 1/2 (or.5) designation. If the 1/2 was to distinguish the 5-spd, how come there is no such thing as an 86 1/2 SE?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Exactly!  That is what I was trying to state in my previous post......there is no distinction between an '86 GT and an '86.5 GT.  It is just a moniker that people give their cars, for whatever reason.<P>The fact that some late '86 GT's got the Getrag in no way differentiates them from the 4-speed versions......ALL can be considered '86.5 because they were not released until June 1986.  <P>This is why I hate the ".5" designator.....it confuses things.  This is evidenced by the above replies that the Getrag '86 GT's are '86.5.  <P>So let me reitterate: <B>ALL</B> '86 GT's are '86.5 GT's and <B>ALL</B> '86.5 GT's are '86 GT's........regardless of which tranny it has.<P>Think of it this way......the auto. tranny was available from the beginning to the end of the '86 production year, right?  So does this infer that a late '86 GT with an auto is not an '86.5? <P>Since everyone seems to think that the only thing that differentiates an '86.5 GT from an '86 GT is the Getrag, then this must be true.  I hope you all see how silly that notion is......the tranny does not affect anything!  A late auto. '86 GT is the same as an early 4-sp. '86 GT which is the same as a late 5-sp. '86 GT which is the same as an ealry auto. '86 GT......they're all the same.....period.||||reg||1
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||10-19-2001||12:44 PM||||Yes & No... I believe the distinction of midyear '86 GT is based on the Bodystyle, not the driveline.  In '85, the GT was a notchback body w/Aero package- in early '86 you could not order a GT model (notchback or otherwise). The SE model was available in '86 with the V6 and the Aero package (minus the wing?)... just no GT badging.  The Fastback '86 1/2 GT was released in June- therefore all '86 GT's really are 86 1/2 GT's.  Irreguardless of the driveline- only body type.<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||10-19-2001||03:00 PM||||MinngreenGT, looks like you've hit on the real reasoning for the "86.5" used to denote 86 GTs!  It was in fact for the new "fastback" bodystyle being released.<P>The 85 SE shared bodystyles with the plain coupe's.  In 86, they took over the previous aero style of the 85 GT (and 84 Indy).  The Gt's were then released well into the 86 model year keeping the aero bumpers while adding the fastback look.<P>The 5-spd tranny in the 86 GT's was just an added bonus!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
